    "reading": {
        "body": "Thomson Reuters positions legal AI as a workflow accelerator for attorneys handling research, litigation, and drafting, with CoCounsel integrated into its Westlaw platform and a newly launched proprietary language model called Thomson announced in August 2026.\n\nThe company frames CoCounsel around what it calls \"Fiduciary-Grade AI,\" a term meant to address the high stakes of legal work product. The product is moving toward agentic workflows capable of multi-step tasks, suggesting a shift from single-query assistance to autonomous handling of complex litigation sequences. A reported case study shows 100% increase in litigation capacity, though this reflects a single firm's claim rather than systematic measurement across the market.\n\nThomson Reuters' launch of its own language model in 2026 signals a move away from reliance on third-party foundation models for legal applications. This matters because legal work involves privileged information, and companies handling such data face pressure to internalize model training and inference. The data does not specify whether Thomson is used in CoCounsel, whether it runs on Thomson Reuters' own infrastructure, or how its compute requirements compare to the company's existing platform operations.\n\nThe data covers one tracked product and does not show Thomson Reuters' full portfolio of AI applications, revenue contribution, or total infrastructure footprint. It does not indicate whether CoCounsel's workflows run on Thomson Reuters' own data centers, cloud services, or both, leaving the relationship between this product and the company's underlying power and building constraints unclear.",
